476
00:27:38,460 --> 00:27:41,700
but it's sort of the long and short of what the resolution was there.

477
00:27:42,660 --> 00:27:45,020
Yeah, I mean, the resolution was, again, as a board,

478
00:27:45,500 --> 00:27:50,580
we made a decision to shut down the bank and at that point we had to move forward.

479
00:27:50,860 --> 00:27:53,900
And so the the only way we could see to move forward

480
00:27:53,900 --> 00:27:57,740
in any kind of reasonable time frame involved reaching a settlement.

481
00:27:58,220 --> 00:28:01,780
And we thought that was the the prudent thing to do,

482
00:28:01,780 --> 00:28:04,020
the financially responsible thing to do.

483
00:28:04,020 --> 00:28:07,500
And the right thing to do is who do you share is for our shareholders and everyone else.

484
00:28:07,500 --> 00:28:11,140
And now it's a it's a shame, by the way, because what gets lost in all this

485
00:28:11,140 --> 00:28:16,420
in the shutdown is, you know, 500 employees lost their jobs and 17.

486
00:28:16,420 --> 00:28:20,780
I don't know how many jobs were lost by the 1700 clients of Silvergate,

487
00:28:21,420 --> 00:28:24,500
but there were a lot of jobs lost, a lot of people harmed.

488
00:28:24,940 --> 00:28:26,980
And in my view, it was unnecessary.

489
00:28:26,980 --> 00:28:29,180
I sit on the other side of the table, right?

490
00:28:29,180 --> 00:28:31,740
I represent the startups that are trying to be banked.

491
00:28:31,900 --> 00:28:35,740
And this is why I got so interested in the issue is because our startups were debanked.

492
00:28:36,180 --> 00:28:41,660
So from my perspective, capital formation startup growth was significantly impaired

493
00:28:42,220 --> 00:28:45,260
by the loss of Silvergate and the general loss of banking.

494
00:28:45,500 --> 00:28:47,740
Yeah, I agree with that.

495
00:28:47,740 --> 00:28:51,420
Were you surprised at all that Silvergate's primary

496
00:28:51,420 --> 00:28:55,140
primary regulators plus the SEC, the securities regulator,

497
00:28:55,580 --> 00:28:58,740
came for Silvergate after the liquidation?

498
00:28:59,180 --> 00:29:01,060
The announcement of the liquidation of the bank?

499
00:29:01,060 --> 00:29:02,940
Yeah, it was the whole thing was surprising to me.

500
00:29:02,940 --> 00:29:09,020
I will say that I felt very naive about the way I had lived my life

501
00:29:09,020 --> 00:29:13,180
up until until a couple of years ago when the regulators came after us,

502
00:29:13,180 --> 00:29:18,620
because it felt the entire process felt very political and very divorced from reality.

503
00:29:19,980 --> 00:29:23,900
And, you know, unfortunately, there's a lot of examples

504
00:29:23,900 --> 00:29:27,100
I wish I could go into were not permitted to.

505
00:29:28,060 --> 00:29:31,820
But it just felt like the whole thing was political and that they were not.

506
00:29:34,540 --> 00:29:36,540
In my view, they were not serving the American people well,

507
00:29:36,540 --> 00:29:39,020
or the people of California well in the way it was approached.

508
00:29:40,220 --> 00:29:44,540
So speaking of not serving the American people well, I want to talk about Diem.

509
00:29:44,540 --> 00:29:46,380
No one ever talks about Diem here.

510
00:29:46,380 --> 00:29:49,820
So I briefly had a footnote about Diem in my last article

511
00:29:49,820 --> 00:29:51,900
because it's just this whole other story.

512
00:29:51,900 --> 00:29:56,300
And then after, you know, after Coinbase started making progress

513
00:29:56,380 --> 00:30:01,340
in their foyers against the FDIC, then I had David Marcus talking about Diem saying,

514
00:30:01,900 --> 00:30:03,420
you know, they were rug pulled.

515
00:30:03,420 --> 00:30:05,500
And that was such a long saga.

516
00:30:05,500 --> 00:30:09,660
But Silvergate's involvement was, I believe, Silvergate was meant to be involved

517
00:30:09,660 --> 00:30:12,380
in the Libra Consortium.

518
00:30:13,420 --> 00:30:16,780
And then Silvergate ultimately bought the Diem assets.

519
00:30:17,580 --> 00:30:20,140
And then that IP was just basically shuttered.

520
00:30:20,140 --> 00:30:21,260
It became worthless.

521
00:30:21,260 --> 00:30:23,900
Will you tell me from your perspective what happened with Diem?

522
00:30:23,900 --> 00:30:28,140
Yeah, I enjoy reading David Marcus's stuff.

523
00:30:28,140 --> 00:30:31,900
And David's exactly accurate from my standpoint and how it was handled

524
00:30:31,900 --> 00:30:33,980
up to the point he ended his involvement.

525
00:30:34,700 --> 00:30:39,900
Where Silvergate came in is, I will say, it goes back to,

526
00:30:40,460 --> 00:30:42,140
maybe you can view this as another rug pull,

527
00:30:42,140 --> 00:30:49,340
but it goes back to January 2021 that the President Biden had a working group

528
00:30:50,540 --> 00:30:53,180
that looked at stablecoins and stablecoin issuance.

529
00:30:53,740 --> 00:30:58,380
And among the things they did is this presidential working group

530
00:30:58,380 --> 00:30:59,820
on financial markets, it was called.

531
00:31:00,460 --> 00:31:06,860
They said stablecoins should be issued by an insured depository institution.

532
00:31:07,660 --> 00:31:12,380
And Silvergate, at that point, we've been in this space for a long time.

533
00:31:12,380 --> 00:31:14,460
We felt like we were sophisticated in this space.

534
00:31:15,420 --> 00:31:20,220
We felt we were uniquely positioned as perhaps the only insured depository institution

535
00:31:20,220 --> 00:31:21,660
that really understood the space.

536
00:31:23,180 --> 00:31:27,980
And so we're familiar with Libra and Diem.

537
00:31:27,980 --> 00:31:32,060
And so ultimately, Silvergate purchased the Diem assets,

538
00:31:32,060 --> 00:31:39,020
intending to move forward and to start issuing a stablecoin through that technology.

539
00:31:39,740 --> 00:31:42,540
Unfortunately, what we found is that the regulators,

540
00:31:42,540 --> 00:31:45,100
again, changed their posture on that.

541
00:31:45,100 --> 00:31:46,540
We were never able to get it approved.

542
00:31:47,500 --> 00:31:53,340
And it ultimately became an asset of the bankrupt estate,

543
00:31:53,340 --> 00:31:54,460
which is what it is right now.

544
00:31:55,660 --> 00:32:01,260
So Silvergate got the impression from the Biden administration at the time

545
00:32:01,260 --> 00:32:04,860
that Silvergate would be a suitable home for the Diem assets.

546
00:32:04,860 --> 00:32:05,580
Yes.

547
00:32:05,580 --> 00:32:09,420
And then that was just radically altered.

548
00:32:09,420 --> 00:32:15,180
I mean, especially in 2023 when the Fed said they didn't want banks to touch stablecoins.

549
00:32:15,900 --> 00:32:18,300
I think that's exactly right.

550
00:32:18,300 --> 00:32:23,340
Now, by the way, I'll mention the Fed has now swapped positions pretty dramatically.

551
00:32:25,020 --> 00:32:27,980
It's been interesting to me that the Federal Reserve Bank was

552
00:32:28,620 --> 00:32:31,900
a major regulator of Silvergate Bank.

553
00:32:31,900 --> 00:32:35,420
We went through this adventure with the Federal Reserve.

554
00:32:35,420 --> 00:32:39,180
And then I see Chairman Powell of the Fed making statements.

555
00:32:40,380 --> 00:32:44,460
And I think his most recent statement said, we're not against innovation.

556
00:32:44,540 --> 00:32:47,500
And we don't want to take any actions that would lead to debanking.

557
00:32:49,100 --> 00:32:55,980
In my view, that's a very significant pivot from where they were in their dealings with us.

558
00:32:56,860 --> 00:32:57,100
Yeah.

559
00:32:57,100 --> 00:33:03,260
I mean, even from the public statements, you know, in Jan 23, the Fed, the FDIC and the OCC

560
00:33:03,260 --> 00:33:04,300
had the joint statement.

561
00:33:04,300 --> 00:33:06,860
They said crypto is the rest of the safety and soundness of banks.

562
00:33:06,860 --> 00:33:09,660
Then I think in August of 23, the Fed comes out and says,

563
00:33:09,660 --> 00:33:12,540
look, banks can do stablecoins if you need a permission slip from us.

564
00:33:13,180 --> 00:33:15,740
Basically implies you can't do stablecoins.

565
00:33:15,740 --> 00:33:17,180
You had side 121.

566
00:33:17,740 --> 00:33:21,900
If you're a bank, you can't really hold crypto assets on behalf of customers.

567
00:33:21,900 --> 00:33:25,340
Basically, I mean, the whole posture was maximum hostile.

568
00:33:25,340 --> 00:33:29,900
And it's remarkable that Jay Powell is changing his tune.

569
00:33:29,900 --> 00:33:31,020
I mean, just a few weeks.

570
00:33:31,980 --> 00:33:33,420
You know, let's administration.

571
00:33:33,420 --> 00:33:37,100
I'm going to say I'm going to say it's thankful that he is, you know,

572
00:33:37,100 --> 00:33:42,060
and I'm not going to appreciate the fact that he's willing to move forward.

573
00:33:42,060 --> 00:33:45,020
Hopefully, and he said nice words and they've said words before.

574
00:33:45,580 --> 00:33:47,980
But I think this time there's more to it.

575
00:33:47,980 --> 00:33:50,620
I think this time there will be some change coming.

576
00:33:50,620 --> 00:33:54,460
I think the Fed will be more responsive, more receptive to innovation in the space.

577
00:33:55,260 --> 00:33:57,660
So, you know, speaking of changes in Washington,

578
00:33:57,660 --> 00:34:01,020
obviously we have hearings in the House and the Senate around debanking

579
00:34:01,020 --> 00:34:04,060
on specifically referencing Operation Choke Point 2.0,

580
00:34:04,060 --> 00:34:06,540
which is what we encrypt to call the whole saga.

581
00:34:08,460 --> 00:34:10,540
It's not the story doesn't just send there, you know,

582
00:34:11,500 --> 00:34:14,940
the new interim chair at the FDIC is said he's going to look into it.

583
00:34:15,980 --> 00:34:18,380
Trump himself said he would end Operation Choke Point.

584
00:34:18,380 --> 00:34:19,740
David Sacks said he would end it.

585
00:34:21,260 --> 00:34:24,380
You know, so it's a market tonal shift in Washington.

586
00:34:24,380 --> 00:34:25,500
I think we will get information.

587
00:34:25,500 --> 00:34:29,500
How optimistic are you that the hearings or maybe congressional investigations

588
00:34:29,500 --> 00:34:35,820
will surface more information about what happened here in terms of regulatory shift?

589
00:34:35,820 --> 00:34:38,860
I'm very optimistic they'll surface more information about it.

590
00:34:38,940 --> 00:34:43,580
And I think there will be some changes, which I think is great.

591
00:34:43,580 --> 00:34:49,660
I think, unfortunately, all the changes that we're going to see deal with, you know,

592
00:34:49,660 --> 00:34:53,100
we're fighting fights that we never needed to fight in the first place from my perspective.

593
00:34:53,100 --> 00:34:55,820
There are some real issues that the crypto industry needs to

594
00:34:56,380 --> 00:34:58,380
figure out the right, you know, the right way to address.

595
00:34:58,380 --> 00:35:01,820
I mean, there's, from my perspective, BSA, AML issues are significant.

596
00:35:01,820 --> 00:35:04,700
We need to figure that out exactly how we're going to make that work.

597
00:35:05,660 --> 00:35:07,500
You know, sanctions, we got to make sure that we're on top of it.

598
00:35:07,500 --> 00:35:08,540
There's a lot of real issues.

599
00:35:09,180 --> 00:35:16,140
This debanking one is is a political issue that was created and didn't need to happen.

600
00:35:16,140 --> 00:35:19,820
In the hearings, if you look at the witnesses, there aren't a lot of bankers, actually.

601
00:35:19,820 --> 00:35:24,220
So there's, you know, Nathan McCauley and Anchorage, they have a charter.

602
00:35:25,660 --> 00:35:26,780
I'm not.

603
00:35:26,780 --> 00:35:31,900
There's one banker from Old Glory Bank, which I don't know much about, actually.

604
00:35:31,900 --> 00:35:34,060
But you're not hearing from Signature.

605
00:35:34,060 --> 00:35:35,580
You're not hearing from Silvergate.

606
00:35:36,460 --> 00:35:37,020
Why is that?

607
00:35:37,020 --> 00:35:42,220
Are there prohibitions on executives of these organizations sharing their story?

608
00:35:42,220 --> 00:35:47,260
It's fair to say that there's no prohibition against them appearing before Congress,

609
00:35:47,260 --> 00:35:50,540
but there's absolutely restrictions on what we can say and can't say.

610
00:35:50,540 --> 00:35:53,660
And I've talked to you just during this conversation we've had,

611
00:35:53,660 --> 00:35:55,580
there's been a couple of times I would like to talk about that.

612
00:35:56,220 --> 00:36:00,700
You have to stay away from what's known as confidential supervisory information.

613
00:36:01,420 --> 00:36:04,540
And it's actually a violation of criminal law to disclose

614
00:36:04,540 --> 00:36:07,420
confidential supervisory information that you receive from your regulators.

615
00:36:08,380 --> 00:36:12,380
That is normally intended to be a shield for banks

616
00:36:12,380 --> 00:36:17,180
so that they can receive candid information to regulators, not have to share it with the world.

617
00:36:17,740 --> 00:36:21,500
But in this case, I think it's being used the other way.

618
00:36:21,500 --> 00:36:25,100
I think it's being used to shield the regulators because anybody who has testified

619
00:36:25,100 --> 00:36:28,780
would have to be concerned about the possibility that they'd violate the law inadvertently.

620
00:36:29,660 --> 00:36:31,100
So that's a big part of it.

621
00:36:31,100 --> 00:36:33,420
I'd say another big part of it is, you know,

622
00:36:33,420 --> 00:36:36,220
many people are still facing lawsuits and other things that they don't want to

623
00:36:36,220 --> 00:36:38,300
create exposure for, or they're continuing in the industry.

624
00:36:38,300 --> 00:36:43,420
And if you're continuing in the industry, you for sure don't want to tick off your regulators.

625
00:36:43,420 --> 00:36:45,660
So I suspect that's what's going on.

626
00:36:46,300 --> 00:36:51,340
And that's the thing that struck me about this is there's such a lack of a paper trail.

627
00:36:51,340 --> 00:36:53,660
There's so few people that are willing to go on the record.

628
00:36:53,660 --> 00:36:57,500
I mean, I'm incredibly grateful that you chose to speak with me today.

629
00:36:57,500 --> 00:37:01,900
I know it takes a lot of bravery to do that, given everything you've been through.

630
00:37:01,980 --> 00:37:07,260
And frankly, the continued threat of settlements, enforcements, et cetera.

631
00:37:07,260 --> 00:37:11,340
I mean, the CSI doctrine makes it hard to speak.

632
00:37:11,340 --> 00:37:15,020
The threat of lawsuits, regulatory harassment makes it hard to speak.

633
00:37:15,020 --> 00:37:19,100
And I'm just hopeful that maybe congressional investigations

634
00:37:19,100 --> 00:37:23,660
will reveal the thought process at the regulators.

635
00:37:23,660 --> 00:37:26,540
But I guess that's to be determined still.

636
00:37:26,540 --> 00:37:27,340
Yeah, I hope that comes out.

637
00:37:27,340 --> 00:37:31,180
One of the things that I'd like to make sure people understand is that

638
00:37:31,180 --> 00:37:37,100
you're extremely unlikely to see a smoking gun memo sent from a regulator to a bank.

639
00:37:37,100 --> 00:37:39,500
That's just not the way bank regulation works.

640
00:37:41,980 --> 00:37:45,500
Bank regulators have such plenary authority over the banks they're regulating that

641
00:37:46,700 --> 00:37:48,540
they merely ask a question.

642
00:37:48,540 --> 00:37:52,540
It's enough to cause most banks to debank someone, right?

643
00:37:52,540 --> 00:37:56,780
If they're just a side-eye glance is almost all it takes.

644
00:37:56,780 --> 00:38:00,220
And it's a shame because this stuff should be done in a more transparent way.

645
00:38:01,180 --> 00:38:05,980
But from the banker's standpoint, you're not going to risk your whole institution

646
00:38:05,980 --> 00:38:07,260
for one customer.

647
00:38:07,260 --> 00:38:10,060
And if the customer is causing any concern at all for your regulator,

648
00:38:10,060 --> 00:38:11,820
the odds are high you're going to drop that customer.

649
00:38:13,020 --> 00:38:16,700
So I have a wish list of changes I would like to bank regulation.

650
00:38:16,700 --> 00:38:18,700
Not that I am an authority on it at all.

651
00:38:18,700 --> 00:38:22,620
But I have been spending a lot of time on it over the last two years,

652
00:38:22,620 --> 00:38:25,980
almost to the day, actually, is when I first published my first article.

653
00:38:25,980 --> 00:38:27,660
And I have things that I want.

654
00:38:27,660 --> 00:38:31,820
But if you were in charge of reforming bank regulation,

655
00:38:31,820 --> 00:38:34,380
what changes would you make to the way it works?

656
00:38:35,660 --> 00:38:36,620
Yeah, you know, it's interesting.

657
00:38:37,500 --> 00:38:41,260
I think the biggest, my biggest takeaway from all this

658
00:38:41,260 --> 00:38:43,660
was simply we were getting away from the way it's supposed to work.

659
00:38:44,460 --> 00:38:46,540
That was the biggest problem we had is that we were not,

660
00:38:47,740 --> 00:38:51,500
from my perspective, the regulators were getting involved in political issues.

661
00:38:51,500 --> 00:38:53,580
Or there are political calls being made in Washington, D.C.

662
00:38:53,580 --> 00:38:55,820
They're being communicated down to the regulators.

663
00:38:55,820 --> 00:38:57,900
So they were actually not, from my perspective,

664
00:38:59,980 --> 00:39:04,220
using their best regulatory judgment and acting in a good faith way

665
00:39:04,220 --> 00:39:05,260
to fulfill their mission.

666
00:39:05,260 --> 00:39:07,340
They were instead being directed to do certain things.

667
00:39:07,980 --> 00:39:11,740
So I don't think the issue is primarily a legislative one.

668
00:39:11,740 --> 00:39:13,980
I think the issue was more congressional oversight,

669
00:39:13,980 --> 00:39:15,340
which I'm glad they're doing it.

670
00:39:15,340 --> 00:39:18,620
A change in administration, which I'm glad there's been some accountability.

671
00:39:18,620 --> 00:39:23,340
And I think, by the way, the crypto industry actually did

672
00:39:23,420 --> 00:39:26,540
instill some accountability by getting involved in elections,

673
00:39:26,540 --> 00:39:29,100
both in the presidential and Senate and other areas.

674
00:39:30,700 --> 00:39:33,900
But I don't have any specific legislative changes I would make.

675
00:39:34,620 --> 00:39:39,180
You made a reference to the fact that a lot of the enforcement work,

676
00:39:39,180 --> 00:39:42,220
the police enforcement work of the government

677
00:39:42,220 --> 00:39:44,460
has been offloaded to financial institutions.

678
00:39:45,980 --> 00:39:48,940
That is absolutely true, but I don't think that's likely to change.

679
00:39:48,940 --> 00:39:51,740
And that's where a lot of this issue is.

680
00:39:51,740 --> 00:39:53,020
That would be a legislative change,

681
00:39:53,020 --> 00:39:54,620
but I don't think it's one that can get through.

682
00:39:55,740 --> 00:39:59,900
Yeah, as long as the Bank Secrecy Act is in place, that's not going to change.

683
00:40:00,460 --> 00:40:04,540
Now, in terms of the Trump administration's ability to tackle this,

684
00:40:04,540 --> 00:40:05,820
it's been a stated priority.

685
00:40:06,540 --> 00:40:09,500
I'm very excited to see both houses looking into it.

686
00:40:09,500 --> 00:40:13,580
Your level of optimism around basically getting to bottom this

687
00:40:13,580 --> 00:40:15,180
or resolving it is fairly high.

688
00:40:15,180 --> 00:40:16,220
Is that fair to say?

689
00:40:16,220 --> 00:40:16,860
Yes.

690
00:40:16,860 --> 00:40:21,900
Yeah, I think the administration is taking aggressive good steps in this area.

691
00:40:21,900 --> 00:40:23,420
I think Congress is looking into it.

692
00:40:24,940 --> 00:40:28,620
It's very difficult to get to the bottom of regulatory matters.

693
00:40:28,620 --> 00:40:33,580
People are pretty good at covering their behind, and they probably did so there.

694
00:40:33,580 --> 00:40:36,780
One thing I'll mention is, as you're looking forward,

695
00:40:36,780 --> 00:40:38,620
we're talking about regulatory actions in the future.

696
00:40:39,420 --> 00:40:44,860
I am confident the federal government is going to focus on what real issues in this space.

697
00:40:45,100 --> 00:40:51,100
I know people in the industry should know that they can't get away with acting like

698
00:40:51,100 --> 00:40:51,980
there's no regulations.

699
00:40:51,980 --> 00:40:53,500
There's going to be a lot of regulatory enforcement.

700
00:40:53,500 --> 00:40:54,940
You got to be careful about everything you do.

701
00:40:54,940 --> 00:40:59,980
And I mentioned BSA, terrorist financing, sanctions,

702
00:40:59,980 --> 00:41:02,220
all kinds of stuff that we need to be very careful about.

703
00:41:04,140 --> 00:41:07,500
But I think the big change coming in the future is not going to come from the federal government.

704
00:41:07,500 --> 00:41:09,660
I think states are going to be stepping up in this space,

705
00:41:09,660 --> 00:41:12,860
and I think the industry needs to be aware of that.

706
00:41:12,860 --> 00:41:20,300
Because for better or worse, right now, crypto has become a political player

707
00:41:20,300 --> 00:41:22,060
and identified as a political player.

708
00:41:22,940 --> 00:41:29,740
And I think what that means is that there will be states where attorneys general and

709
00:41:30,460 --> 00:41:34,700
governors and others, regulators, will seek to make political points

710
00:41:34,700 --> 00:41:36,300
by going after the crypto industry.

711
00:41:36,300 --> 00:41:37,340
And they do have the authority.

712
00:41:37,340 --> 00:41:38,700
There's a lot of consumer protection.

713
00:41:38,700 --> 00:41:41,340
There's a lot of state blue sky security laws.

714
00:41:41,340 --> 00:41:43,340
There's other ways states will be involved.

715
00:41:43,340 --> 00:41:46,460
So I don't think that the change in government in D.C.

716
00:41:46,460 --> 00:41:51,180
is some kind of panacea for the industry where it's all going to be roses.

717
00:41:51,180 --> 00:41:52,460
But I do think it's better.

718
00:41:52,460 --> 00:41:53,500
It's absolutely going to be better.

719
00:41:53,500 --> 00:41:55,340
It's making a huge, it's going to be a big change.

720
00:41:55,900 --> 00:41:58,380
And I think it's going to enable U.S. companies

721
00:41:58,380 --> 00:42:00,300
to get back to doing what they should be doing,

722
00:42:00,300 --> 00:42:04,940
which is building good companies, providing good services.

723
00:42:06,380 --> 00:42:08,460
So I think it's taken a couple of worries off the table

724
00:42:08,460 --> 00:42:09,820
and maybe it's added one or two others.

725
00:42:10,380 --> 00:42:13,820
To my understanding, this is one of the first times you've spoken on the record

726
00:42:13,820 --> 00:42:16,140
since the dissolution of Silvergate.

727
00:42:16,700 --> 00:42:20,300
If you wanted to set the record straight on something that we haven't covered today,

728
00:42:21,100 --> 00:42:24,620
what's kind of a misconception about the bank that you want to resolve?

729
00:42:24,620 --> 00:42:26,700
It frustrates me that people think that somehow

730
00:42:26,700 --> 00:42:30,700
Silvergate was in some ways in cahoots with FTX or anybody else

731
00:42:31,260 --> 00:42:32,780
and was a bad actor in this.

732
00:42:32,780 --> 00:42:37,820
I am 100,000% convinced that Silvergate was not a malicious or bad actor.

733
00:42:37,820 --> 00:42:39,500
And that's a message it's got to get out.

734
00:42:39,500 --> 00:42:43,500
I'm also extremely frustrated by the fact that short sellers,

735
00:42:44,140 --> 00:42:49,260
sometimes working with Congressional and Senate officeholders,

736
00:42:49,260 --> 00:42:54,380
are able to just say outrageously false statements with complete impunity.

737
00:42:54,380 --> 00:42:56,540
And we need to figure out a way.

738
00:42:56,540 --> 00:42:58,220
If you ask for a regular or legal change,

739
00:42:58,220 --> 00:43:02,940
I would love to see short sellers face some liability

740
00:43:02,940 --> 00:43:08,380
for making malicious and false statements in kind of a reverse pump and dump

741
00:43:08,380 --> 00:43:11,580
because they're trying to make money by driving the stock of prices down.

742
00:43:11,580 --> 00:43:12,540
That I would love to see.

743
00:43:13,260 --> 00:43:14,300
But that'd be the biggest thing.

744
00:43:14,940 --> 00:43:17,980
Other than that, I think people should know that from my perspective,

745
00:43:17,980 --> 00:43:23,020
Silvergate was a bank that tried to do it right and I think did do it right.

746
00:43:23,020 --> 00:43:26,700
Not perfectly, but did do it right and was, in my view,

747
00:43:28,060 --> 00:43:30,300
killed unnecessarily by regulators.

748
00:43:31,660 --> 00:43:33,900
And there's a different world where that didn't need to happen.

749
00:43:34,540 --> 00:43:39,180
And it makes me sad that, you know, that institution is gone.

750
00:43:39,180 --> 00:43:40,780
And as I mentioned, the employees are gone.

751
00:43:40,780 --> 00:43:44,380
The investors lost their money or a big chunk of their money.

752
00:43:44,380 --> 00:43:47,980
And all the customers are out scrambling, still looking for banks, many of them.

753
00:43:47,980 --> 00:43:50,140
Yeah. And, you know, I actually forgot to ask this,

754
00:43:50,140 --> 00:43:55,660
but like in terms of the future for common or even preferred shareholders,

755
00:43:55,660 --> 00:43:57,420
is there a case against the government?

756
00:43:57,420 --> 00:43:59,100
Is there a regulatory takings case?

757
00:43:59,100 --> 00:44:00,620
I've heard this idea batted around.

758
00:44:01,580 --> 00:44:06,060
Is there any kind of prospect of recovery beyond the assets that exist in the bank?

759
00:44:06,780 --> 00:44:08,300
All that's in the bankruptcy court right now.

760
00:44:08,300 --> 00:44:12,940
There isn't any lawsuits as an asset of the bankruptcy, you know, the estate.

761
00:44:14,620 --> 00:44:17,980
And it will not be my decision how to pursue that.

762
00:44:17,980 --> 00:44:23,580
A U.S. trustee or a bankruptcy court will be approving any action like that.

763
00:44:24,540 --> 00:44:26,700
So I don't know whether it's going to go forward,

764
00:44:26,700 --> 00:44:29,900
but to the extent there's a claim, it belongs to the estate.

765
00:44:30,780 --> 00:44:33,660
In terms of your next steps, I mean, we haven't talked about that at all.

766
00:44:33,660 --> 00:44:35,500
You know, what's your plan?

767
00:44:35,500 --> 00:44:37,580
I'm excited about this industry.

768
00:44:37,580 --> 00:44:42,220
I'm excited about, you know, the sort of little niche role I've managed to carve out in it,

769
00:44:42,220 --> 00:44:47,260
which is to help help companies deal with regulation and help financial,

770
00:44:47,260 --> 00:44:49,580
you know, help banks deal with it.

771
00:44:49,580 --> 00:44:51,420
I'm I don't know what my next step.

772
00:44:51,420 --> 00:44:52,220
I'm active now.

773
00:44:52,220 --> 00:44:54,700
I'm on, you know, fully busy.

774
00:44:54,700 --> 00:44:56,300
My plate is full right now.

775
00:44:56,300 --> 00:44:57,820
I don't see any critical change coming.

776
00:44:58,220 --> 00:45:02,780
But I'm excited by what the next few years are going to bring.

777
00:45:02,780 --> 00:45:05,820
And I think some of the companies that I'm working with

778
00:45:05,820 --> 00:45:07,740
are going to be quite large and quite successful.

779
00:45:07,740 --> 00:45:10,300
And it's a ton of fun.

780
00:45:10,300 --> 00:45:14,780
I also think, by the way, that somebody else is going to recreate Silvergate Bank.

781
00:45:14,780 --> 00:45:15,900
It's going to happen.

782
00:45:15,900 --> 00:45:17,580
And it's a shame it's not going to be Silvergate,

783
00:45:17,580 --> 00:45:21,020
but that somebody else is going to be offering those same services to this industry.

784
00:45:21,020 --> 00:45:22,540
And it's going to be very successful at it.

785
00:45:23,180 --> 00:45:26,860
And, you know, assuming that they're careful and I'm sure they will be

786
00:45:26,940 --> 00:45:29,260
and that the regulators are prudent,

787
00:45:30,700 --> 00:45:34,140
we'll end up with a safe, secure financial institution

788
00:45:34,140 --> 00:45:35,500
that's offering all these services.

789
00:45:35,500 --> 00:45:38,220
And I'm hopeful that Silvergate 1700 customers

790
00:45:38,220 --> 00:45:41,580
and all the other customers out there are able to get get bank accounts,

791
00:45:41,580 --> 00:45:44,140
get back into the economic system of the United States.

792
00:45:44,140 --> 00:45:47,660
Well, I mean, despite the ordeal that you and your colleagues went through,

793
00:45:47,660 --> 00:45:52,060
like I'm very heartened to see that you are still optimistic about the industry.

794
00:45:52,060 --> 00:45:55,100
I don't know if I would be, but it's really great to see.

795
00:45:55,740 --> 00:45:56,620
It wasn't the industry.

796
00:45:57,420 --> 00:46:00,620
I don't feel like that, you know, that anything that went wrong at Silvergate

797
00:46:00,620 --> 00:46:01,980
was not the crypto industry.

798
00:46:01,980 --> 00:46:06,460
I mean, you know, obviously, FTX would have been better if that hadn't happened.

799
00:46:06,460 --> 00:46:12,300
But, you know, the strengths of the industry so far outweigh any weaknesses.

800
00:46:12,300 --> 00:46:17,100
And I think the issue that Silvergate had was honestly a change in policy

801
00:46:17,100 --> 00:46:21,980
with the regulators that, you know, my view was done in the dark,

802
00:46:21,980 --> 00:46:25,340
in a back room somewhere without proper public discussion.

803
00:46:25,340 --> 00:46:28,460
And, you know, that's now been corrected.

804
00:46:28,460 --> 00:46:30,620
The new president was elected in part on that.

805
00:46:30,620 --> 00:46:33,900
And so I'm very optimistic, actually, where this is going.

806
00:46:33,900 --> 00:46:36,860
Well, Mike, I can't express my gratitude enough.

807
00:46:36,860 --> 00:46:39,260
Thank you so much for coming on and sharing your story with us.

808
00:46:39,260 --> 00:46:41,420
And it's been such a pleasure to have you on.

809
00:46:42,300 --> 00:46:42,940
Great. Thank you.

810
00:46:42,940 --> 00:46:45,580
I thank you. I should have said early for the report you've done in space.

811
00:46:45,580 --> 00:46:49,820
It's been, I think, spot on from my perspective and very helpful.

812
00:46:49,820 --> 00:46:51,980
Well, here's to things getting better.

